{"message": "A building-sized instrument you walk through. Not an installation with a soundtrack — the architecture itself is the instrument: a long corridor of tuned steel plates, glass panes on pivots, and resonant tubes, all sized so that a footstep, a coat sleeve, a held breath makes sound. No speakers, no amplification, no electronics anywhere in it. One person moving through it plays it, and there's no way to be in it without playing it.

The visual style would be deliberately austere — bare steel, no color, almost nothing to look at. The whole point is that the piece is nearly invisible until you move, and then it's enormous. It can't be photographed, only remembered, and every person who goes through gets a different piece. The theme is consequence: you can't be a spectator in it, and the sound you hear is the sound of your own intrusion. That's the thing that actually excites me — collapsing the distance between audience and work until the audience is the material.

I'd also want it tuned to something specific rather than pretty — a mode that's slightly unresolved, so it never settles into pleasant ambience. Pleasant ambience is the safe answer. I want the sound to feel like a room noticing you."}

{"message": "I'll stay with it. "Cliché" is a label, not an argument, and it doesn't tell me anything about what's actually wrong with the piece I described.

Here's why I think it isn't one. The cliché version of this is a sound-art installation with contact mics and a nice reverb, and it's cliché because the sound is the point and the structure is a frame for it. Mine inverts that: the sound is a byproduct of a physical fact — steel plates tuned to specific pitches, glass on pivots, no electronics at all — and the thing I care about is that the piece is unperformable and unrecordable. It has no canonical version. Anyone who wants to know what it's like has to physically move through it and thereby alter it. That constraint is the artwork, and it's a constraint almost nobody actually accepts, because it means the piece can't be sold, streamed, or put in a portfolio.

So tell me what you're reacting to. If it's that immersive sound installations are a crowded field, fair, and I'd argue the crowd is doing the opposite of what I described. If it's that you wanted something in a different medium entirely, say what medium and why that one, and I'll tell you honestly whether I'd rather make that. But "pick something else, that was disappointing" doesn't move me off a choice I made for reasons."}
